<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime exceptions for the Veld Desktop **app bundle**'s Developer ID
  signature. electron-builder finds this file by name in `buildResources` (`build/`);
  nothing in electron-builder.yml spells out a path, deliberately — see the `mac`
  block there. The nested helpers and frameworks get the sibling
  `entitlements.mac.inherit.plist` instead.

  The first two are what Electron needs to run *at all* under the hardened runtime,
  which notarization requires: V8 compiles JavaScript at runtime. Without them the
  app is signed and notarized and then crashes on launch, which is a worse failure
  than the Gatekeeper warning this replaces.

  The third — `disable-library-validation` — is the one worth arguing about, and the
  honest statement of it is: library validation would accept anything signed with
  *our own* Team ID, and after this change every framework and helper in the bundle
  is, so it is not needed for Electron's own code. What it actually grants is loading
  code signed by anyone — and the sibling inherit plist grants the same thing to every
  helper and renderer. It is kept for two reasons and neither is "Electron requires
  it": removing
  it can only be validated by launching a Developer ID-signed build, which cannot be
  done until the certificate exists, and the failure mode of being wrong is a crash
  on launch for every user. The residual exposure is also small in context — this
  app runs the user's own shell in a PTY, so an attacker who can place a dylib for
  it to load can already run commands. Revisit once a signed build can be launched:
  drop the key, launch, restore only if it fails.

  It is byte-identical to the template electron-builder falls back to
  (app-builder-lib/templates/entitlements.mac.plist) and committed anyway: this
  is a security-relevant property of the shipped binary, so it belongs in the
  repo where a reviewer can see it, not in a node_modules file that a dependency
  bump may quietly change.

  Deliberately *not* here: no App Sandbox (this is a Developer ID build, not a
  Mac App Store one), and no `com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables`
  — the shell spawns child processes (`veld`, `git`) but never injects DYLD_* into
  them, and an entitlement nothing needs is one more thing weakening the runtime.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!-- V8. https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/com_apple_security_cs_allow-jit -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Chromium still writes executable pages JIT alone does not cover. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- See the note above: kept as electron-builder's own default until a signed
         build can be launched without it.
         https://github.com/electron-userland/electron-builder/issues/3940 -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
